 ## Overview
-Compositing is the process of combining multiple color and opacity contributions into a final pixel value, a step required exclusively for semi-transparent volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of integrating optical properties along a viewing ray to produce a physically plausible image [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Alpha Blending](5750-alpha-blending.md), which typically blends two distinct layers, or [Pre-Integration Lookup](2854-pre-integration-lookup.md), which approximates integration via texture lookups, standard compositing explicitly accumulates samples along the ray using the emission-absorption model [S3, unofficial].
+Compositing combines multiple contributions along a ray, a step required only for semi-transparent rendering [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of projecting a 3D density field onto a 2D image plane while preserving internal structure and translucency, eliminating the need for binary classification [S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Alpha Blending](5750-alpha-blending.md), which typically blends two distinct layers, or [Pre-Integration Lookup](2854-pre-integration-lookup.md), which approximates integration via texture lookups, standard compositing explicitly accumulates samples along the ray using the emission-absorption model [S3, unofficial].
 
